tlsresume — provenance note
============================

Kept as a module-local NOTICE (same placement as the sibling `dtls` module,
which is likewise a self-contained TLS/DTLS 1.3 crypto module not folded into
the root `NOTICE`).

Provenance
----------

tlsresume — clean-room implementation from RFC 8446 (TLS 1.3): §4.2.11 (the
`pre_shared_key` extension), §4.2.11.1 (obfuscated ticket age), §4.2.11.2
(the PSK binder), §4.6.1 (`NewSessionTicket`), §7.1 (the resumption-PSK key
schedule), and §8 (anti-replay). This is a public, open IETF specification —
not a copyrightable work (merger doctrine; see CONVENTIONS.md §5) — so no
third-party-source NOTICE entry is strictly required for the RFC citation
alone.

This module additionally names a real design reference consulted for the
exact CLIENT-side label/wire shapes a compliant SERVER implementation must
match (no source copied): `ianic/tls.zig` (github.com/ianic/tls.zig), a
third-party pure-Zig TLS 1.3 implementation vendored elsewhere in this
authors' toolchain (as a `zig fetch`-style package dependency, not committed
into this repository). Specifically studied:

  - `src/transcript.zig`'s `setPreSharedSecret` (derives the resumption
    handshake secret via `hkdfExpandLabel(..., "resumption", ticket_nonce,
    ...)` then `Hkdf.extract`), `resumptionSecret` (`"res master"` label),
    and `pskBinder`/the standalone `pskBinder_` test helper (the "res
    binder" -> "finished" -> HMAC chain, validated in that file's own test
    against the same RFC 8448 §4 vector this module's `psk.zig` KATs use).
  - `src/handshake_client.zig`'s `Options.SessionResumption.Ticket`
    (`lifetime`/`age_add`/`nonce`/`identity` field shape from parsing a
    live NewSessionTicket) and `Ticket.obfuscatedAge` (the RFC 8446
    §4.2.11.1 obfuscation arithmetic this module's `replay.zig` mirrors on
    the server side), plus `makeClientHello`'s `preSharedKey`/
    `preSharedKeyBinder` wire-writing sequence (confirms the binder is
    computed over the transcript hash of the ClientHello truncated to
    exclude the binders list itself).

No source was copied from `ianic/tls.zig` — only the derivation-label
sequencing and wire-field shapes were studied, the same way this
collection's `dtls` module studied (without copying) Zig std's internal TLS
client. The vendored copy examined during this scaffolding pass carried no
bundled `LICENSE` file alongside its source; the upstream project
(github.com/ianic/tls.zig) is publicly described as MIT-licensed, but that
was not independently re-verified against a license file during this pass —
flagged here rather than asserted with false confidence.

RFC 8448 ("Example Handshake Traces for TLS 1.3", IETF, public) supplied the
known-answer test vectors this module's tests assert against (fetched
directly from https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8448 for this pass, not
copied from any other implementation's test suite).

`std.crypto.tls.hkdfExpandLabel`, `std.crypto.kdf.hkdf`,
`std.crypto.auth.hmac`, `std.crypto.aead.aes_gcm`, and
`std.crypto.timing_safe` are used via their public APIs (MIT, part of Zig
itself) — see `psk.zig`'s module doc for the specific recon finding that,
unlike `dtls` (DTLS 1.3), `hkdfExpandLabel`'s hardcoded `"tls13 "` prefix is
directly correct for this module's plain-TLS-1.3 target, not a gap to work
around.

No GPL/LGPL/AGPL source was consulted or copied anywhere in this module.

**Status: crypto core implemented + KAT-validated.** `psk.zig` (PSK
derivation + binder) is byte-exact against RFC 8448 §4; `stek.zig`'s
`seal`/`open` (AES-256-GCM ticket sealing, key-id-bound AAD), `replay.zig`'s
`StrikeRegister.checkAndMark` (single-use anti-replay), and `select.zig`'s
`selectPsk` (server-side identity selection + const-time binder verify) are
real and tested (34/34, Debug + ReleaseFast). What this module deliberately
does NOT own: the TLS handshake state machine / flight engine — it is the
engine-agnostic resumption crypto + wire-codec seam only (the engine passes
in transcript hashes and clocks), the same scoping as `dtls`.
